    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Has a Taste of the e-MTB World with the RN0 Motor-Assisted Bicycle\n1958 Chevy Impala Begs for a New Home, All Impressive Until You Open the Hood\n19 photos\nPhoto: eBay seller fasttyrone\nThey are already original once. The old saying makes perfect sense when checking out this 1958 Impala, a car that’s already in tip-top shape and doesn’t look like it requires any further investments. The only problem hides under the hood, where the original engine is long gone.\n1958 Impalas are fantastic collectibles. They are the ones that started Chevrolet’s full-size revolution, so collectors would pay high dollars to get their hands on a specimen that hasn’t been altered in any way. An unrestored survivor costs more than a house, but the demand for these cars is still at impressive levels.\nAs you can see in these photos, this Impala is a gorgeous car that sports a super-clean setup pretty much anywhere. You don’t need to worry about typical classic car issues, such as rust, so you can get this Impala, park it in your garage, and only take it out on sunny Sundays.\nPhoto: eBay seller fasttyrone The 1958 Impala wasn’t just the car that put Chevrolet back on the map. It indeed propelled the GM brand to the leading spot in the United States car market, finally pushing Ford to second place, but it landed just at the right time to spearhead Chevrolet’s V8 push.\nThe Bel Air introduced several V8 engines during the Tri-Five era, and the 283 that saw daylight in 1957 was the icing on the cake. This was the engine that ended up powering most Chevrolet cars sold in the late ‘50s and the rest of the ‘60s, including the Impala.\nBut the Impala debuted in 1958 with a brand-new 348 big-block configuration, whose Tri-Power ended up becoming one of the most desirable engines on a full-size Chevrolet . It remained the biggest engine until the debut of the 409 in 1961 - the same year when the first Super Sport saw daylight - but big-block replacements only gained traction in 1962.\nThe 348 is the engine to get on a 1958 Chevrolet Impala, especially if your goal is having a top specimen that still boasts the original - or, at least, the correct - setup according to the 1958 model year.\nPhoto: eBay seller fasttyrone Unfortunately, this is why some collectors would end up disappointed when checking out this beautiful engine. Instead of a 348 in charge of putting the wheels in motion, this car is powered by a 350 crate engine. Sure, it does its job, probably quite nicely, but I don’t think that it can replace the famous 348 in any way.\nThis is all the information we’re getting about the engine, so if you’re interested in a purchase, my recommendation is that you call the seller and discuss the rest of the details after you ask all your questions. There’s a new transmission in there, so if you’re specifically interested in the powertrain, there’s a lot to discover on this Impala.\nThe vehicle is currently at its third owner, and it’s unclear when it received the refresh that you can now see in the photos. It runs and drives properly, and it also comes with power steering and power brakes.\nPhoto: eBay seller fasttyrone My recommendation is that you arrange an in-person inspection because it’s unclear how original it continues to be. The owner uses terms like “original” and “barn find,” but without digging deeper into its past, it’s difficult to tell what exactly happened to this Impala before it received the overhaul we can now admire today.\nThe Impala wasn’t an affordable car back in the day, as the hardtop could be had for at least $2,586, while the convertible started at $2,724. However, installing more powerful engines and additional options increased the price significantly. For example, the Rochester fuel-injected engine - the 283 that was introduced in 1957 and which generated 290 horsepower this year - carried an extra price tag of $484.\nBuyers who wanted to install air conditioning on their Impala had to pay $468 more, while a four-speed transmission was available for $188. The Turbo-Glide four-speed automatic carried an extra price of $231, so eventually, a highly optioned Impala ended up costing more than $4K.\nPhoto: eBay seller fasttyrone So how much should this Impala cost? The owner believes $65,000 is a fair price, but they also enabled the Make Offer option on eBay, meaning they are interested in further negotiations if you’re committed to a purchase.\nThere’s no mention of any paperwork that might be included in the sale, so if you’re a collector who’d pay more dollars just to get the original build sheet and documents, this could be another reason to walk away. You’ll get a title, but that’s pretty much everything.\nThe car is parked in Benton, Louisiana, and considering its shape, you wouldn’t need transportation to take it home, therefore allowing you to save the extra shipping costs. The listing expires in 6 days, so you have enough time to call the seller and ask all your questions.\nVia // eBay\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nImpala Chevrolet Chevrolet Impala 1958 Impala\nAbout the author: Bogdan Popa\nBogdan keeps an eye on how technology is taking over the car world. His long-term goals are buying an 18-wheeler because he needs more space for his kid’s toys, and convincing Google and Apple that Android Auto and CarPlay deserve at least as much attention as their phones. Full profile\nUP NEXT VW Teases New Hot Hatch, Concept Appears To Be Based on the ID. Polo GTI",
